---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Architecture notes"
  description="The boundaries behind Veasel Code's V-native server and client API."
>
  <section class="doc-page page-grid">
    <p class="eyebrow">FIELD GUIDE / 02</p>
    <h1>
      Keep the core
      <br />
      <em>close to the work.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Veasel is server-first. Clients use a versioned API; the V application
      owns session state, storage, event history, bounded workspace tools, and
      the provider loop.
    </p>
    <div
      class="architecture-map"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Terminal client connects to the loopback API, which connects to session storage, workspace tools, and the V agent runtime"
    >
      <div class="map-node">
        <small>CLIENT</small>
        <strong>OpenTUI</strong>
        <span>TypeScript</span>
      </div>
      <div class="map-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
        →
      </div>
      <div class="map-node map-core">
        <small>BOUNDARY</small>
        <strong>HTTP · SSE</strong>
        <span>127.0.0.1</span>
      </div>
      <div class="map-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
        →
      </div>
      <div class="map-node">
        <small>RUNTIME</small>
        <strong>V + SQLite</strong>
        <span>sessions / events / tools</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>State change first, event alongside it</h2>
      <p>
        Session state and its creation event are written in one database
        transaction. The event stream carries monotonically increasing IDs so a
        client can reconnect from a cursor and recover what it missed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Permission before effect</h2>
      <p>
        The model can propose a bounded replacement for one text file. Veasel
        saves its diff without touching the workspace. In the normal TUI flow,
        the user opens that diff before a one-time approval can apply it;
        application verifies the reviewed file snapshot and uses an atomic
        replacement. The loopback API has no application token, so same-user
        processes can call those routes. Shell execution is not available, and
        interrupted writes are not replayed automatically.
      </p>
    </div>
    <a
      class="text-link"
      href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md"
    >
      Open the implementation plan <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
    </a>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
